This folder holds three things from Musashi, https://github.com/kstenerud/Musashi:
m68000-valid.hex, a table generated from its disassembler, and mc68000/ and
mc68040/, its self-checking test programs. All are covered by Musashi's
licence, at the end.

The `musashi` feature's differential harness of the 68010 dialect
(src/conformance/musashi.rs, src/test/differential.rs) compiles Musashi's own
source, which scripts/fetch-musashi.sh fetches into tests/musashi/fetched, which
git ignores, at the commit below; build.rs compiles it for the host's tests
only, with M68K_EMULATE_TRACE on, and nothing fetches or compiles it without
the feature. It runs Musashi as a 68010 beside s68k, one instruction at a time,
and compares the registers, the memory and the frames, with the places where
Musashi is not the 68010 listed with their reasons in the harness.

m68000-valid.hex
================

What it is
----------

Which of the 65,536 first words of an instruction Musashi's disassembler
accepts as 68000 instructions: the answer of

    m68k_is_valid_instruction(word, M68K_CPU_TYPE_68000)

for every word from $0000 to $FFFF. It is the legality oracle of the decoder's
test `the_legal_first_words_are_musashis_68000_words` in
src/machine/decode.rs, which lists the words where the decoder departs from it
and why.

Format: 256 lines of 64 hexadecimal digits. Line N (from 0) covers the words
$NN00 to $NNFF; each digit covers four consecutive words, the most significant
bit the lowest word; a set bit is a valid instruction.

Where it comes from
-------------------

Generated on 2026-10-28 from Musashi, https://github.com/kstenerud/Musashi,
at commit 313ebf1bd9f4d0d93341eb5ce21fd8a119e9dbdd (2026-03-08), by compiling
its m68kdasm.c, unchanged, with the small program below, outside this
repository, and running it with no argument. The data is a table of facts
computed by that code; no part of Musashi's source is in this repository.

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include "m68k.h"
    unsigned int m68k_read_disassembler_16(unsigned int a){return 0;}
    unsigned int m68k_read_disassembler_32(unsigned int a){return 0;}
    unsigned int m68k_read_disassembler_8(unsigned int a){return 0;}
    int main(void) {
        for (unsigned line = 0; line < 256; line++) {
            for (unsigned digit = 0; digit < 64; digit++) {
                unsigned value = 0;
                for (unsigned bit = 0; bit < 4; bit++) {
                    unsigned word = line * 256 + digit * 4 + bit;
                    value = (value << 1)
                        | (m68k_is_valid_instruction(word, M68K_CPU_TYPE_68000) ? 1 : 0);
                }
                putchar("0123456789abcdef"[value]);
            }
            putchar('\n');
        }
        return 0;
    }

    gcc -O1 -I<Musashi> -o valid valid.c <Musashi>/m68kdasm.c && ./valid

